00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.600
OK, so just to give a sense of
the video compression or video

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:07.340
codecs out there, so you might
have heard about these terms

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:12.360
here or these codecs here,
H.264, H.265, VP9 and so on.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.270
So these are different
compression algorithms

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:17.990
or compression
standards for video.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:21.190
H.264 is actually the most
common one which you would see.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.590
So if you are typically
encoding or typically having

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.500
a video anywhere, most
likely it is going

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.416
to be encoded using H.264.

00:03:30.416 --> 00:03:33.800
H.264 is today around
15 to 20 years old,

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.847
but it's still widely used
just because, I mean, it

00:03:36.847 --> 00:03:37.430
is reasonable.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.740
At the same time,
it's something which

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.440
is supported on almost
all the devices, right?

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.110
And so these are basically
different generations.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.720
H.265 is the newer
one which came maybe

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.520
around five to 10 years back.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.910
And H.266 is something which
is in active development,

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:57.630
so you might not have H.266
today on your devices yet,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:02.360
but it's something
which is being added.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.820
So this is basically
a set of video codecs.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.700
And the goal is,
obviously, to take a video

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.650
and convert it to bits, right?

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:10.610
So that's the goal.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.430
And today, we are going to try
to understand how they really

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.860
work.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.930
OK, so one thing which I
wanted to actually just

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.350
to emphasize that how
important video compression is.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.400
I mean, the thing is that
video compression is, first,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.780
so important today.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.780
And at the same time
it's so intensive

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.840
that video codecs have been--
both encoding and decoding

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.430
have been put on silicon.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.610
So people have put in
the effort and the money

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:42.560
to put a reasonably small
but still quite important

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.310
media engine.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.970
For example, this is a
snapshot from the M2 chip which

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:52.370
Apple has, where, I
mean, part of the chip

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.820
has a portion which does video
encoding, video decoding,

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:56.520
and so on.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.840
So it just tells us how
important video compression is,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.380
and at the same time,
also how costly it

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.440
is because video
encoding, decoding

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.110
does take a bit of an
effort on your machine,

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:12.500
and so putting it on
silicon is something which

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:13.850
is what makes it tractable.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.570
And so there have been n
number of PhDs on this,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:21.950
just to make the video codec
work, but at the same time,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.770
I mean, optimize it
to every millisecond

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.260
and so on, and then run
it on chips and so on.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.382
So we are not going to discuss
this aspect of the codec,

00:05:31.382 --> 00:05:32.840
trying to keep it
high level today.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.673
But definitely, there
is a lot of literature

00:05:34.673 --> 00:05:39.140
out there if you want to try to
understand how video codecs are

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:43.280
implemented on chips and make
it to make them run efficiently

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.260
and so on, right?

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.930
All right, so OK, so
let's try to understand

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.280
how video compression works.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:53.670
But even before then,
let's go to the basics.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.680
What do we actually
mean by a video?

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.110
And the answer is quite simple.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.960
I mean, I assume
everyone knows this.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.260
But the way in which we
interpret video or one way

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.180
to think about the
video is that it's

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.393
a sequence of frames
or images, right?

00:06:08.393 --> 00:06:10.560
So it's a sequence of images
which we have captured,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.080
and that's where
the name comes from,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.070
motion pictures, and
pictures and motion in a way.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:21.660
And so this is basically a set
of sequence of images which

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.480
is what we want to compress.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.110
Now, this is something which
I found quite interesting when

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.210
I was preparing for
this lecture last year,

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.270
which was that, actually,
the first video was captured

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.140
on Stanford's campus, and it
was Leland Stanford's Horse.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.370
You might have seen this
video or image at some point,

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.070
and really, they call it a
video but it was basically

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.880
a few images of a horse,
maybe 12 images or something

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:49.900
which they clicked.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.230
And the reason for
this video capture

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.660
was to really know
if horses' legs are

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.600
in the air for a fraction
of a second or not.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.180
I mean, that was
the first video,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.120
so it's nice that we are
having this talk at Stanford

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:04.885
given the history.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.690
OK, so let's take a
look at, I mean, what--

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:17.610
so this is just a sample
video which we will

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.970
work with during the lecture.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:25.110
I mean, if you look at, it's a
jockey video, again, a horse.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.600
Just given the history, we are
going to take a horse video

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.650
and try to compress it.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.180
Now, whenever you
look at a video,

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:33.030
there are a couple of things
to understand or look at,

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:37.650
and the two numbers are
the FPS and the frame size

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:38.845
or the resolution, right?

00:07:38.845 --> 00:07:40.470
The frame resolution
is just the number

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.770
of pixels along the height
and width dimension,

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:47.820
and here, this is a 720p frame.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.030
At the same time,
FPS is the frames

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.700
per second, so that's the
number of frames which you have

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.490
or which the video has
when it was recorded,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:57.780
which is 30, right?

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.348
So it just tells you
the amount of, I mean,

00:08:00.348 --> 00:08:01.890
images which are
there in this video.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:02.915
It's quite incredible.

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.290
I mean, today,
most of the videos

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.977
are also captured at 60 FPS,
so this is slightly old,

00:08:07.977 --> 00:08:09.810
but it just tells you
that you are basically

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:13.230
capturing relatively large
images 30 times a second.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.570
And if you are recording a
video for five, 10 minutes,

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.820
then it's going to be
a crazy big file which

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.530
needs to be compressed, right?

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.890
OK.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.090
So far so good?

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.830
Any questions as of now?

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:26.640
OK, cool.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.740
So let's try to understand
this video a bit more.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.198
So what I'm going
to do is that I'm

00:08:30.198 --> 00:08:35.429
going to use a utility
called media info.

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.230
You can just install
on your machines,

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.220
and look at this video.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.270
So this is a raw .y4m file
is a raw video encoding file,

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:46.320
and we can look at this encoded
file to see what is happening.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.810
And so you can see
the usual suspects.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.780
There is the
resolution here, which

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.180
is one quality,
cross 720, and so on.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.340
There's 30 FPS as we discussed.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.250
The color scheme is
actually YUV 4:2:0.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:01.350
I believe Pulkit
might have discussed

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.310
this in one of the lectures
on why we use YUV 4:2:0

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.190
color scheme, right?

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.550
At the same time, one very
interesting fact to note

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:16.860
here is this 332 megabits
per second bit rate.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.500
So that is actually
the file size.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.080
If you are going to
record, just store

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.710
this video as it is, raw
video, one second, it's

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.230
going to take 332 megabits--

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.148
I mean, divide by
8 to get megabytes.

00:09:30.148 --> 00:09:32.190
But it just tells you that
it's quite incredible.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:36.420
I mean, if I have to, I mean,
stream this video to my home,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.540
I mean, I have maybe 100
megabits per second Comcast net

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:40.620
at home.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:41.670
It's not going to play.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.795
I mean, the video is just
not going to play, right?

00:09:43.795 --> 00:09:45.210
So it just tells
us that, I mean,

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:48.120
we really need
video compression.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.720
I mean, our infrastructure
can't really handle videos

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:52.980
in the raw format.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.380
I mean, this is also
a much smaller video.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.120
Today, we watch
Netflix at 4K and so

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.120
on so it's going to be
much bigger than this.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.320
So it just tells us that we need
video compression quite a bit.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.450
All right, OK.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.850
So let's see.

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:13.600
I mean, let's try to use
H.264 to compress this video

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.700
and see what file
size we get, right?

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:19.300
So I'm putting these
commands at the bottom.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.900
So the most common
way you will use--

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:26.283
I mean, apart from just
using some of the UIs,

00:10:26.283 --> 00:10:27.700
if you're using
command line, then

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.010
you can use FFmpeg which
is a very common utility

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.620
to work with video, not
just compression, anything

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.910
related to video processing.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:37.120
And here, I'm
using at x264 which

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.780
is one specific encoder
for the H.264 codec.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:47.080
And I'm encoding this video at
this parameter called CRF20.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.760
You probably might
have heard about this

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.670
when JPEG compression
was discussed,

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.970
but CRF basically tells
you the quality parameter

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.490
or the quality level,
and 20, typically,

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:00.400
corresponds to
human imperceptible.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.120
So this is at a level where
humans typically don't really

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:05.750
perceive.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.600
I mean, there are no distortions
which you will perceive,

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.200
and so this is at this
particular compression level,

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:12.800
right?

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.530
And at this level, what we
see is that already, I mean,

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.870
the compression which we
get is quite incredible.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.030
I mean, it's almost 50X.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.060
The video using
H.264, we already

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.997
get it down to 6.2
megabits per second,

00:11:25.997 --> 00:11:27.080
which is quite incredible.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.910
And I'm not sure if
you can see anything.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:32.000
Let me play the video, if
you see any weird artifacts,

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:32.900
at least I don't.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.190
So it's relatively good.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:38.570
I mean, probably there might be
some artifacts in the fountains

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:43.940
and so on, but it's
relatively good,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.860
depending on the
application, so OK.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.300
So let's try to go ahead.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:51.920
I'm going to use, again, the
same command, and x264 codec

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:53.780
to encode it at CRF40.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:56.690
So this is a more aggressive
compression level,

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:58.043
and you will see that.

00:11:58.043 --> 00:11:59.960
I mean, we get much
better compression, right?

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.660
It's 0.8, it's even 6X lower.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:06.520
But at the same time, I mean,
if I play the video, some of you

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:08.270
might notice, some of
you might not notice

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.180
but there are a bunch of
artifacts which crop up.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:15.140
So I don't know if maybe
at the head of the jockey,

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:18.350
there are some weird blocky
artifacts which come up,

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:19.700
and which are--

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.410
even here, you can
see on the legs,

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:22.850
there are a lot
of artifacts which

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.920
come up at this
compression level, right?

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.670
So I mean, of course,
at least using x264,

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.280
there is some limit, but
we know that at least we

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:36.020
can get this 50X compression
using x264, so OK.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:40.990
Let's try to understand
how we can get this, right?

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.150
OK, so now, as I
mentioned, we are

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.490
going to use all our
past knowledge, whatever

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:47.740
you learned in this course
to try to achieve this.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.380
And I know that, I mean,
everyone is at this point

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.390
familiar with how
JPEG works, and so

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:54.795
we are going to use
image compression to go

00:12:54.795 --> 00:12:56.170
towards video
compression, right?

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:59.860
And so image compression will
use just as a black box or as

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:02.590
a utility function
in the beginning,

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.350
and see what we can
do with that, right?

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.520
So, OK.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.670
Maybe a quick question.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:13.290
What is the first
thing you would do?

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.415
Let's say you have an image
compressor implemented.

00:13:15.415 --> 00:13:16.860
You have the JPEG
compressor which

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.140
you implemented in
your homework and you

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.600
want to apply that
for a video, right?

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.760
What is the first
thing you would do?

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.670
Any thoughts?

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:26.770
Yeah.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.480
Compress each
frame individually.

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:29.260
Exactly.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.380
Yeah, so the simplest idea,
just from definition, a video

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.120
is a sequence of
images, so that just

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.520
try to compress each frame
independently, right?

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:40.600
And if you do that, and
we can in fact do that,

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:44.800
and that's actually called
I-frame compression.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.650
You will see this term thrown
around in video compression

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:48.220
literature.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:52.900
It just means that if a frame
is compressed independently

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.930
as an image, it's
called a I-frame, right?

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.090
And so we can just compress
each of these frames

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:02.050
independently like an image, and
that's why I-frame compression.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.060
And so let's see
how good it does.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:08.200
So you actually see that
on this video actually,

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:11.800
if you just do image-based
compression, just individually,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.957
compress every frame, then
we get a very, I mean,

00:14:14.957 --> 00:14:15.790
amazing compression.

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:18.850
It's already within
almost half of the--

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:21.010
I mean, it's not
as good as if you

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:26.060
use the H.264 codec, but just
I-frame compression of H.264,

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.060
you get 9 megabits per
second as compared to six,

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:30.500
what we got there, right?

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:33.920
So I mean, the
results could differ.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:37.160
I'll show a couple of more
videos where there is not

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.040
as much motion, and we'll see
that if we just compress them

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:43.513
as it is, then the compression
is much worse with I-frames.

00:14:43.513 --> 00:14:45.680
But on this video, because
there is a lot of motion,

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:48.200
we see that just I-frame,
just this simple idea which

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:50.810
you mentioned, we are
able to get within 9

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:52.310
megabits per second, right?

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:54.470
And so now, the next
question is, OK, how

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:58.100
can we go from here towards
the 6 megabits or even lower?

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:02.650
So that's the next
question to answer, OK?

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:04.420
All right, so again,
this is the video

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:05.680
compressed using the I-frames.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:07.970
It's relatively good.

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:10.678
OK, so I'm going to
show a couple of images

00:15:10.678 --> 00:15:12.220
and I'm going to
toggle between them.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:16.300
And let's see if there
is another thought

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:20.800
on how to go forward, so yeah.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:22.780
I mean, the key
idea or the hint is

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.990
that compression is all
about removing redundancy.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:29.080
Wherever you see redundancy,
wherever you see correlation,

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:31.000
you have to correlate
these things, right?

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.880
So any, again, thoughts
on what we could

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.160
do to improve the compression?

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.510
OK, one hint, OK, yeah?

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:47.550
There's a correlation
between the frames

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.970
so if we do something like
encode the first frame,

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:52.230
then we can use that.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.960
We can say the second
frame shares a lot

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.070
of the same components.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:57.420
Yeah, that's right.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.340
I mean, both of these
frames if you look,

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.010
they share a lot of
common information, right?

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.780
I mean, it's quite obvious.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.780
So if we have the Frame-0
encoded as an image,

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:10.110
then if you look at frame
one, then most of it

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:11.700
is just shifted
to one direction.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.820
So we could basically just
copy paste most of it as it is.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:16.290
And if there is a
small difference,

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.260
that's what we could encode
using an image and so

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:19.960
on, right?

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.360
So that's what we actually do.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.780
So the overall pipeline actually
looks something like this,

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.090
that you take Frame-0, Frame-1.

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:31.290
So this Frame-0 is
actually the encoded frame.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.130
I'm calling it Frame-0.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:34.170
We find the motion.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:37.200
So here, there is some motion.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.940
The logo is static.

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.180
That's a different story.

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:43.720
And then we basically
find the motion.

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:47.020
And then to compute the
Frame-1, what we can do

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:50.200
is that we can apply this
motion on top of this Frame-0

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.270
to get a compensated frame.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:56.290
And then we can encode this
residual, the difference,

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:59.260
separately as an image.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:00.580
Is this clear?

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:04.359
So this is the broad idea of
how video compression works,

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:08.589
that you compress one frame
as it is and then encode

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:10.000
the difference in
a separate way.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.670
And this mainly works
because our frame rate,

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:14.500
FPS is quite fast.

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:16.930
I mean, one by
30th is quite fast

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:19.690
and so the motion is
typically much smaller.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:22.810
If you look at two frames
separated with one by 30th

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:24.640
of a second,
typically, they're not

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:28.240
going to be different
by quite a bit.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:29.590
So, all right.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.900
So this is actually what
is called P-frame coding.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.300
So we spoke about I-frame
coding where every frame

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:38.560
is independently encoded.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.740
This is the P-frame
coding where the P-frame

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:42.820
is the predicted frame.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.552
So what you do is that
you first encode the--

00:17:45.552 --> 00:17:47.260
let us say we start
with the first frame.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.150
It's encoded as
an image, then you

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.030
encode the second
frame, the P-frame

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.043
dependent upon the first
frame, and then the third frame

00:17:55.043 --> 00:17:56.710
dependent upon the
second encoded frame,

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:57.865
and so on, so on, so on.

00:17:57.865 --> 00:17:59.740
And at some point,
obviously, your prediction

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.510
is not going to be great because
every time you are doing this,

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:06.340
there is some noise added to it
and it might become a bit more

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:10.030
expensive so you refresh
by adding a new I-frame.

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:12.010
Or if the scene changes,
let's just say--

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:13.648
I mean, when the
horse is running,

00:18:13.648 --> 00:18:15.190
something else comes
into the middle,

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.650
then you can add
another I-frame.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:22.630
And so that's how,
typically, the P-frame works.

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:25.330
Another key one thing
which I wanted to mention

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:26.380
is this keyint.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:29.920
So the keyint, you will
also see this term.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.220
It implies the distance
between two I-frames,

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:36.610
so that's the distance between
two I-frames or key frames,

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.520
which is six in this case.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:41.140
Is this fine?

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:42.890
OK, cool.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:44.680
So this is the
overall block diagram

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:48.730
for our IP-coding, which
is that, I mean, we first

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.700
find the motion, we
encode the motion,

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:55.330
and then we apply this decoded
motion on top of the previously

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:56.410
reconstructed frame.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.170
And that gives us the
motion compensated frame.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:02.260
And then we take the difference
and then encode this difference

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:04.630
as an image compression, right?

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:06.700
OK, so now maybe
another question

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.138
is that, I mean, in the
previous I-frame coding,

00:19:09.138 --> 00:19:10.930
we also had image
compression block, right?

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:14.230
It was basically operating on
a same image again and again.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.810
In this case, we have a
much more complicated thing.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.240
There is this motion
compensation and so on.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.430
And again, there is image
compression block here.

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:23.230
So why do you think
this should do better?

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:26.320
I mean, even though we still
have the image compression

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.150
block here, right?

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:32.140
So why do you think
this does better

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.480
or this should do better?

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:35.830
Yeah?

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.020
The residual should be very low.

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:40.330
Yeah, good answer.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.630
It should be mostly space or--

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:44.500
Yeah.

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:46.030
Some artifacts.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:47.320
Yeah, that's a good answer.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.780
So we know that
image compression

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:51.400
depends on the image itself.

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.077
If the image is
simple, then you are

00:19:53.077 --> 00:19:54.910
going to be able to
compress it much better.

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:57.940
If there is a lot of things
happening in the image,

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.740
then it's going to
take a lot of bits.

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.900
And so the residual,
as you mentioned,

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.810
if it's simple, if it's
all blank most of it,

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:07.870
then if we use something
like JPEG to compress it,

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.120
the residual we should be
able to do much better.

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:11.440
So that's the idea.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.870
We are trying to extract
out all correlation

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:18.010
before we use our image
compression techniques here.

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:20.860
And that's what most
of the video codecs do.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:25.160
OK.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:36.220
All right, so let's now
try to discuss how this--

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:38.518
I mean, we already
know this block.

00:20:38.518 --> 00:20:40.810
We already know how the image
compression works, right?

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:41.810
I mean, we looked at it.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.020
So let's first try to
focus on this part here.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:48.160
How do we really find the motion
and how do we encode it, right?

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.070
So the encoding part, I'm not
going to discuss as much today,

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.890
but it's similar to what
you have seen in the class

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:56.410
but let's look at how do
we really find the motion.

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:59.080
And so in H.264 and most
of the traditional codecs,

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:03.370
the simplest idea is that, it's
again, based on correlation.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:07.570
So you take the reference frame,
you take a reference block,

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:12.610
and then try to move
this block, try to check

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.350
whether where this
block matches, right?

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:16.127
And wherever this
block matches, you

00:21:16.127 --> 00:21:19.030
know the location of that
block, that's the motion.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.670
And so that's the
simple idea based

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.350
on block matching algorithm.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:25.840
By the way, there are
a lot of good resources

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.118
from a past course at Stanford.

00:21:28.118 --> 00:21:29.660
Unfortunately, it
doesn't run anymore

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.960
but Bernd Girod has a lot of
good resources on the website

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:35.000
so do look at that if
you want to really go

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.970
into the nitty gritty
details of video coding.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:39.890
So that's basically
the broad idea.

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:40.730
It's quite simple.

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:41.870
You just take a block.

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:43.700
Again, it depends
on the algorithm.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:46.430
What block size do
you want to take

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:48.557
and how do you want to do
the searching and so on?

00:21:48.557 --> 00:21:50.390
But the broad idea is
that you take a block,

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:53.240
you try to match it, and
wherever you get a good match,

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:56.090
that's your motion, OK?

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:57.260
And, OK.

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:01.760
So maybe here is some
examples from, again,

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.110
if we apply the motion.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:05.780
As you can see here,
there is some motion.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:06.958
Most of it is static.

00:22:06.958 --> 00:22:09.500
There is some motion in these
branches while they are moving,

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:14.000
and we take the
residual and so on.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.730
Now, again, I mentioned there
are different strategies

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:20.990
to do the block search but
I'll just mention a few.

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:24.710
So here is full searching
where you can basically

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:27.590
search across the entire
image, which is obviously

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:28.700
way more expensive.

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:31.970
But the way in which most of
the times people implement this

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:33.410
is hierarchical searching.

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:35.990
So what you will
do is that, I mean,

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:37.670
if you want to do
block matching,

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.890
you will actually start
from the lowest level.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:43.490
You will downsample
your frame by say, 4X.

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:45.110
You will do block
matching there.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:48.080
Once you find a
coarse match, then

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:50.240
you will, again, sample
your frame and find

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:51.780
matches there and
so on and so on.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:53.360
So you can do this
as a multi scale,

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:57.770
just to make the problem
more tractable and solvable.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:22:59.840
And this is very similar to--

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:01.730
I mean, a lot of the
video processing which

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:06.860
you will see today, I mean, in
most of the video processing

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:09.560
or even image processing
uses image pyramids which

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.110
is very similar to this idea.

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:13.070
Is this fine?

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:18.280
People understand how the
motion vectors are found?

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:20.980
Yeah, and then once you
have found these vectors,

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:25.660
you can just encode
the differences are

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:30.620
your motions per block, and then
you will encode these motions,

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:31.710
right?

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:34.330
OK, so maybe let's take
a pause and I actually

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:40.610
want to show you a
sample encoded video.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.920
So we are going to use
actually something called

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:46.090
[? Stream-I. ?]

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:54.040
OK, so even before we
look at our jockey video,

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:56.740
because the horse video is
slightly more complicated,

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.390
I'm going to look at this one.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:04.360
So this is a slightly simpler
one because it's just smaller.

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:12.160
OK, let me-- yeah, so
this is a software which--

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:15.100
what it is doing is that it's
looking at the encoded file

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:18.760
and it's just trying to display
all the encoded information

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:21.700
in a much different way, right?

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.190
All right, so here,
what it's showing

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:28.390
is that it's showing the
number of bits used per frame.

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:30.520
So this bar chart
actually tells the number

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:34.840
of bits used per frame
and every frame here.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:39.730
So maybe one quick
question is that, you

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:41.230
see that there is
this high bar here

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.063
and there are these
smaller bars after that,

00:24:43.063 --> 00:24:44.860
and again, high bar
here and smaller bars.

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:46.425
Any idea what is happening?

00:24:46.425 --> 00:24:47.800
Why are some of
these frames have

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.258
such high motion
while some of them

00:24:49.258 --> 00:24:51.650
just don't have as
much high motion?

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:54.520
I mean, sorry, not motion,
the file size, the bits used

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:59.020
for that frame is so high in
some cases while not as much

00:24:59.020 --> 00:24:59.890
in other cases.

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:02.065
Any idea?

00:25:02.065 --> 00:25:04.390
What are these frames?

