We saw that.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:39.510
Just I-frame compression
on this video

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:41.940
takes 9 megabits per
second while I and P

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:44.280
takes 6 megabits per
second so it doesn't help

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:46.410
as much in this case, right?

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:48.180
And yeah, it's quite thin here.

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:51.990
It's just because we are relying
on the P-frame, the residual

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.670
to be sparse for
good compression.

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:57.960
But if there is a lot of motion
and if the motion is weird,

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:01.140
then we might not be able to
predict the next frame as well

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:02.910
based on the previous
one, and so then you

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:05.970
will be using a lot of bits.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:11.665
And I think if we view the
