00:31:08.193 --> 00:31:10.110
So I mentioned mediainfo
is a quick tool which

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.900
you can use to view things.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:16.950
And I showed the
raw file before,

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:18.870
so now we can look
at the encoded file.

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:20.730
And you can see
a bunch of things

00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:23.670
here like this
advanced video codec.

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:27.120
I mean, advanced video codec
is another name for H.264.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:29.790
It used to be advanced
10 years back,

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:34.110
it's not as advanced today but
yeah, anyway, that's the name.

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:40.260
And so you will see AVC also put
in the files at various places.

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:43.560
And again, another thing
which you see, the stream size

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:47.850
is 3.16 megabits.

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:51.990
Let's see, where is the--

00:31:51.990 --> 00:31:54.510
OK, so this is the bit rate.

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:56.760
Bit rate is 6 megabit
per second, which

00:31:56.760 --> 00:31:58.360
is what we had seen earlier.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:02.417
And you see a lot of settings
here, encoding settings.

00:32:02.417 --> 00:32:04.000
And you will see
that this is metadata

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:05.320
which is the encoding settings.

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:08.680
So FFmpeg internally
does a lot of options

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:11.260
while encoding this file and
you have the manual control

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:12.670
to change these things.

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:15.370
For example, it uses cabac
which is adaptive arithmetic

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:19.930
coder instead of Huffman,
or it will use LookAhead

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:25.210
while finding the motion because
in case of P-frames, one frame

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:28.840
also impacts the next future
8 P-frames, for example.

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:30.820
And so you need to
look ahead to see

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:32.240
what the motion is and so on.

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:33.910
So you can do all
sorts of optimizations

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:36.490
to improve the
compression and that

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:40.457
makes the encoding much slower,
but sometimes better, right?

00:32:40.457 --> 00:32:42.040
Yeah, so there's
just a lot of options

00:32:42.040 --> 00:32:43.270
which you can look into.

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:47.050
We are not going to try to
understand anything of here

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:50.365
today, but it's quite
interesting to see that.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:54.010
Cool.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.830
OK, so another one thing
which you could ask

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:59.770
is that why restrict us
to this kind of a coding

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.830
where we just
start with an image

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:04.955
then we encode one
frame independently,

00:33:04.955 --> 00:33:07.330
and then we encode the next
frame conditioned on that one

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:08.470
and then go on go on?

00:33:08.470 --> 00:33:11.230
Instead, what we
could do is that--

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:12.897
because this is extrapolation.

00:33:12.897 --> 00:33:14.980
You are taking one frame,
you are extrapolating it

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:17.410
to the next frame, and then
encoding the difference.

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:20.590
Typically, in real life, we
know that interpolation works

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:23.710
better, so why not
encode, do it that way?

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.170
So let's just have
one image frame.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:31.270
Let's predict this P-frame
based on I-frame like here.

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:34.420
But then the
intermediate two frames,

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:37.450
let's interpolate between
frame number one and three,

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:38.920
one and four, right?

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:42.190
And that, I mean, potentially
because this P-frame

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:44.413
is further away, it's
three or four frames away,

00:33:44.413 --> 00:33:46.330
there will be much more
motion and the P-frame

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:47.830
could cost us more bits.

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:51.610
But the frame number
two and three here

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:53.570
could basically cause--

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:54.820
I mean, could be much simpler.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:57.730
I mean, the bit rate to use
them could be much simpler, much

00:33:57.730 --> 00:33:58.810
smaller, right?

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:03.340
And so this is called
B-frame encoding.

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:05.740
I mentioned P stands
for predictive coding,

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:09.610
the B stands for bilinear
coding because it references

00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:12.850
both left to right directions.

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:14.530
Yeah, so again,
we could do this.

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:18.130
And in fact, if you
encode your video, I mean,

00:34:18.130 --> 00:34:21.250
when you upload your video on
YouTube or most of the times,

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:25.360
videos nowadays are actually
encoded using B-frames.

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:26.485
In fact, I'll come to that.

00:34:26.485 --> 00:34:28.652
There's something called
hierarchical B-frames which

00:34:28.652 --> 00:34:30.670
is what is used,
but B-frames is what

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:34.630
is used for encoding
most of the videos which

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.929
you watch on Netflix or
YouTube and so on, right?

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:39.670
And the main reason
why this works better

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.650
is just interpolation
or extrapolation,

00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:43.929
that interpolation is
a much simpler problem.

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:46.540
So if you have one frame
here and one frame ahead,

00:34:46.540 --> 00:34:48.535
you can interpolate
between them quite well.

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:53.630
Cool.

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:57.200
I mean, we know that it should
lead to better compression

00:34:57.200 --> 00:35:00.290
but let me show you that.

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:01.850
Again, we'll do that later.

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.730
But any thoughts on when
you might want to use--

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:08.360
I mean, sometime, in some cases,
I and P frame coding might

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:10.610
be better in some applications.

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:13.910
Any thoughts on when you might
prefer I and P-frame coding

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:15.620
versus IPB frame coding.

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.870
Yeah, OK, so the hint is
that, I mean, in compression

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.060
in this class,
most of our concern

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:29.820
has been just better
compression, right?

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.670
But at the same time in
real life, a lot of times,

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:35.820
there are other constraints
like, I mean, speed,

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:36.960
latency, and so on.

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:39.480
And so those constraints
come into the picture.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:42.330
For example, with
the B-frame, let

00:35:42.330 --> 00:35:45.600
us say you want to start
looking at this video, right?

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:48.302
Now, if you want
to, the frame one

00:35:48.302 --> 00:35:50.760
will get decoded quickly because
it's an independent image.

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:52.920
But to decode frame
number two, you first

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:55.128
need to decode frame
number four and then decode

00:35:55.128 --> 00:35:55.920
frame number three.

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:57.420
So it adds to this latency.

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:00.060
It adds to one frame
latency, which in some cases

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:02.640
is OK, in some cases
it might not be.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.040
For example, if you are
doing real time video calling

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:09.540
with someone, then, I mean,
it might be prohibitive.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:12.390
I mean, first of all, you don't
know what is this future frame.

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:16.133
You can't just wait for n number
of frames until someone talks

00:36:16.133 --> 00:36:16.800
and then encode.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:20.160
Otherwise, the Zoom talk
is going to be delayed.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:22.500
Everyone is going to
receive delayed frames.

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:23.670
So it's just not possible.

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:29.670
And so you will see that Zoom
chats or whatever, Google Meet,

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:31.830
Facetime, all of those
use I and P style

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.980
coding, but at the
same time, Netflix

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:38.640
or any video-on-demand uses
B-frame because there, it's OK.

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:39.660
There, it's fine.

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:41.040
You already know
the entire video

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:43.020
and so you can encode
using B-frames.

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:45.990
And so you might have seen that
if you are watching on YouTube,

00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:47.820
if you try to seek
to some point,

00:36:47.820 --> 00:36:49.590
it might not start
from that exact point.

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:51.270
It will go somewhere
towards the left

00:36:51.270 --> 00:36:53.040
or towards the right
or somewhere, right?

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:56.100
So what happens is that it syncs
it towards the closest I-frame

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:59.280
because it can't start decoding
at some arbitrary points.

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.710
It has to start decoding
from an I-frame.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:03.360
And so if you go
to YouTube and try

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:06.717
to seek to some arbitrary
point, it will maybe not

00:37:06.717 --> 00:37:07.800
start at that exact point.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:10.560
It will go somewhere
left or right.

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:14.970
Yeah, is the B-frame
clear right now?

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:16.860
All right, yeah.

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:20.160
So this is what I was
mentioning that B-frames

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:25.260
is low latency settings
for video conferencing.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:28.950
I plus P plus B-frames, it's
also called random access mode,

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.290
again, because it
allows you to--

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:33.420
I mean, it's a slightly
misnomer but it allows

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:36.810
you to get much better
compression, at the same time,

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:39.390
seek at various points.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:41.820
At the same time, you will see
I-frame compression is also

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:45.780
useful, and it's actually useful
in video editing softwares.

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:49.950
So you have used ProRes or I
mean, if you have one of you

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:53.670
is a YouTuber, then there are
these mezzanine formats which

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:54.720
encodes the video.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:57.960
And the reason is not to
save space on the file,

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:01.080
it's just that to make this
editing at least possible.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:04.410
If the file size is few
gigabytes, just editing

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:05.670
an image is going to be crazy.

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:07.380
So what it does is
that you compress

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:09.150
every image independently,
and so if you

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:11.260
want to edit this video,
you can load one frame,

