00:42:58.930 --> 00:43:03.400
in there, other P-frames
which are in there.

00:43:03.400 --> 00:43:05.650
Yeah?

00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:06.150
Cool.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:14.785
All right, so we can also
look at our jockey video here.

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:25.027
Yeah, I mean, again,
it's slightly weird

00:43:25.027 --> 00:43:25.860
because it remains--

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:31.270
even if I am encoding
with B-frames,

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:34.137
the encoder decides that because
there is a lot of motion,

00:43:34.137 --> 00:43:36.220
it doesn't make sense to
encode using the B-frames

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:38.920
and so you'll see that
at least in this case,

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:40.540
I'm seeing that
it's all P-frames.

00:43:40.540 --> 00:43:43.810
We'll see a go ahead.

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:46.630
In the first thing, it's
all P-frames, right?

00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:50.560
Here, it's again, P-frames.

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:57.940
And then maybe--

00:43:57.940 --> 00:44:01.060
OK, there are some
B-frames here, right?

00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:03.220
So even if we specify
to the encoder

00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:06.520
that I want to use IPB
frames, it need not use them.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:08.560
If the motion is a lot
more, it might just

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:11.020
think that it's probably
OK to use I and P frames

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:11.665
in this case.

00:44:14.750 --> 00:44:19.720
OK, cool.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:23.890
So just to summarize
the motion encoding,

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:28.852
I mean, basically, the
block based searching

00:44:28.852 --> 00:44:30.310
and the encoding
which we discussed

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:32.950
is extremely efficient.

00:44:32.950 --> 00:44:34.750
But at the same
time, it's very axis

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:37.630
aligned so very similar
to BPG and JPEG.

00:44:37.630 --> 00:44:39.730
It will produce these
blocky artifacts

00:44:39.730 --> 00:44:43.300
which you will see if your video
sometimes your bandwidth drops

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:45.970
on Zoom or something, you
will see a lot of weird blocky

00:44:45.970 --> 00:44:49.510
artifacts and that's mainly
because of these algorithms.

00:44:49.510 --> 00:44:52.240
And so these algorithms
also need these deep

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:54.370
blocking algorithm
filtering which

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:59.290
is part of the loop, which does
some post-processing to make

00:44:59.290 --> 00:45:00.970
the video look slightly better.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.440
But, I mean, a lot
of times it just

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:04.060
doesn't work because
there is just

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:09.320
so much of blocky artifacts that
it typically might not work.

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:12.090
Cool.

00:45:12.090 --> 00:45:15.150
All right, so I just
wanted to quickly touch

00:45:15.150 --> 00:45:20.700
base on how there have been
in parallel literature ongoing

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.160
from traditional video
coding towards machine

00:45:23.160 --> 00:45:24.810
learning-based
video coding, right?

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:28.320
And we did this exercise in
the class where you took JPEG

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:31.050
and transformed it into a
learned image coder, right/

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:33.780
So we could think of
in that same lines.

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:36.510
And maybe the first thing
which you could do here

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:40.110
is that let's keep this
motion-based block alone.

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:42.990
We know that we have
learned image compressor.

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:47.220
Let's just try to replace the
traditional image compression

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:50.280
pipeline here using
ML-based coder, right?

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:53.190
And so that obviously could
give you much benefits.

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:56.640
But at the same time,
if the entire thing

00:45:56.640 --> 00:45:58.620
is trained machine
learning-based,

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:00.390
then it could also
give benefits.

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:04.932
And the reason is that the
flow quality might be better.

00:46:04.932 --> 00:46:06.390
The motion quality
might be better.

00:46:06.390 --> 00:46:08.580
Instead of using
block by block, you

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:10.500
could have motion which
is much more smoother

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:13.650
and that can give you much
better benefits, right?

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:15.330
So I just wanted to show some.

00:46:15.330 --> 00:46:18.270
So this is, again, a
few years old video.

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:22.050
So if you use
block-based searching,

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:23.610
your motion is
something like this,

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:27.300
but learned techniques can
have much smoother video just

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:30.960
because, I mean, they
are a bit more stronger

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:33.780
and they can have a bit more
biases built into it, right?

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:36.570
And that, typically, can
lead to less artifacts.

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:39.660
So for example, here
is H.265 which is

00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:41.550
one generation ahead of H.264.

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:44.430
And you see that there are a
bunch of these blocky artifacts

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:48.870
in here at lower bit rates,
while ML-based codecs are

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:54.020
a bit more smoother as
compared to H.265, right?

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:56.320
Yeah.

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:57.820
And so there has
been a lot of work.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:00.760
Again, this is a few years old.

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:03.070
There has been a
lot more progress,

00:47:03.070 --> 00:47:07.810
not as much on the PSNR-based
metrics but on other metrics,

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:13.060
but you see that the ML-based
codecs are already much better

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:14.020
than--

00:47:14.020 --> 00:47:17.860
the purple curve is the H.265
and it's much better than that.

00:47:17.860 --> 00:47:22.840
AV1 is something a newer
codec which is already

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:26.860
part of some of the machines
but not adopted everywhere,

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:28.960
but it's also better than
that, and things have

00:47:28.960 --> 00:47:30.430
improved since then as well.

00:47:32.960 --> 00:47:35.270
At the same time,
I mean, one thing

00:47:35.270 --> 00:47:37.340
which we saw in the
image compression class

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:41.518
is that it's not very easy to--

00:47:41.518 --> 00:47:43.560
it's very easy for a
machine learning-based codec

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:46.590
to optimize for a more
complex metric, right?

00:47:46.590 --> 00:47:48.510
And so if we take
the metric as, say,

00:47:48.510 --> 00:47:51.420
MSSIM, which is a more
perceptual-based metric,

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:54.030
then ML-based codecs have
a much higher advantage

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:55.680
and they can do much better.

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:57.190
And so that's what we see here.

00:47:57.190 --> 00:47:59.640
And so that's where
maybe the future is

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:04.260
going towards, that I mean,
video-based coding, what

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:08.200
we know now, H.264,
H.265 is simple.

00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:11.640
It works, but at the same
time, ML-based codings

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:12.760
can do much better.

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:14.760
But at the same time,
there is a lot of tradeoff

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:18.300
because I mentioned that
the another constraint is

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:20.640
that video decoding
or video encoding

00:48:20.640 --> 00:48:22.410
needs to run in real time.

00:48:22.410 --> 00:48:27.270
You are running these things 30
FPS or 60 FPS on your machines,

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:29.820
and so it needs to
run extremely fast.

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:32.910
And so that's the reason why
you can't really rely on,

00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:36.270
I mean, using a machine learning
model which is crazy big.

00:48:36.270 --> 00:48:39.000
One of the large language
models, large video models,

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:41.290
it might not work
on your machines

00:48:41.290 --> 00:48:44.470
because it definitely won't
run in real time today.

00:48:44.470 --> 00:48:46.780
But of course, with
a bit more of effort,

00:48:46.780 --> 00:48:50.290
it's possible to run it
real time at some point.

00:48:50.290 --> 00:48:54.610
Yeah, cool.

00:48:54.610 --> 00:48:58.030
All right, thank you.

