00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:51.840
OK, so concluding
just a short sort

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:54.210
of summary of what we
have learned together.

00:50:00.800 --> 00:50:03.740
So yeah, so in the early
days, a couple of months

00:50:03.740 --> 00:50:07.160
ago, we started looking
at very simple IID data.

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:09.920
We did some theory the
first few lectures.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:15.620
We learnt about entropy and then
we define prefix-free codes.

00:50:15.620 --> 00:50:19.610
We looked at a few specific
examples of prefix-free codes,

00:50:19.610 --> 00:50:22.490
Huffman codes,
arithmetic codes, rANS.

00:50:22.490 --> 00:50:27.230
And as you might have
seen, since then,

00:50:27.230 --> 00:50:29.540
both in the first half
and in lossy compression,

00:50:29.540 --> 00:50:32.480
these are fundamental
building blocks, right?

00:50:32.480 --> 00:50:34.730
Even today, Kedar
mentioned cabac

00:50:34.730 --> 00:50:38.180
which is very efficient
arithmetic coder.

00:50:38.180 --> 00:50:43.220
And so all of these are used.

00:50:43.220 --> 00:50:45.470
Even if your data is
not IID, right, you

00:50:45.470 --> 00:50:47.420
can transform your
data in various ways

00:50:47.420 --> 00:50:50.810
get to something that is almost
IID, and then use one of these,

00:50:50.810 --> 00:50:51.560
basically.

00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:53.600
And all of these have
different tradeoffs

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:56.240
in terms of how close
they get to the entropy

00:50:56.240 --> 00:51:00.890
and how efficient they are
in encoding and decoding.

00:51:00.890 --> 00:51:01.400
Right.

00:51:01.400 --> 00:51:05.540
And then the other thing we
looked at in the first half

00:51:05.540 --> 00:51:07.610
was, fundamentally--
also in the second half,

00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:09.402
but in the first half
we looked at entropy,

00:51:09.402 --> 00:51:12.500
which is the fundamental
limit of compression.

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:15.350
This is one thing I really liked
about compression in the years

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:21.470
I've worked on it, is that the
theory really helps you out

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:22.370
in practice.

00:51:22.370 --> 00:51:24.500
You can use the
theory, especially

00:51:24.500 --> 00:51:26.960
for lossless
compression, I've seen.

00:51:26.960 --> 00:51:30.410
You can start with the theory,
make a model for your data,

00:51:30.410 --> 00:51:32.360
get some numbers,
and then actually

00:51:32.360 --> 00:51:34.880
compare what you're getting
in practice with the numbers,

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:36.740
and that gives you
a lot of insight.

00:51:39.535 --> 00:51:41.410
And not only that,
actually some of the tools

00:51:41.410 --> 00:51:46.060
from information theory are used
beyond just information theory.

00:51:46.060 --> 00:51:51.040
Because if you remember the
fundamental result for lossless

00:51:51.040 --> 00:51:55.720
compression where we showed that
no compressor in the world can

00:51:55.720 --> 00:51:58.150
do better than entropy,
it's a very strong result.

00:51:58.150 --> 00:52:01.810
It's non-trivial to prove
results where any algorithm

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:05.230
in the world cannot do
better than something, right?

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.470
And it's used in various places.

00:52:08.470 --> 00:52:10.360
You are actually
getting a glimpse

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:14.885
and define the best role
problem, the homework for,

00:52:14.885 --> 00:52:15.385
right?

00:52:20.870 --> 00:52:23.780
And the last thing we saw in
the first half, the last three

00:52:23.780 --> 00:52:26.480
lectures, basically,
was non-IID data

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:30.740
or what we call
real data, right?

00:52:30.740 --> 00:52:35.090
And so one key idea we learned
was, we looked at entropy rate,

00:52:35.090 --> 00:52:36.680
we looked at
conditional entropy,

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:38.870
and then we learned that
a good predictor gives you

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:41.960
a good compressor, and a
good compressor gives you

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:43.700
a good predictor, right?

00:52:43.700 --> 00:52:47.030
And we saw some different
types of models.

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:49.490
We saw kth order
adaptive coders.

00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:52.310
Many of you, some of the
projects you are doing,

00:52:52.310 --> 00:52:55.940
there are a few projects on
context tree weighting, EPM,

00:52:55.940 --> 00:52:59.210
which are more advanced
predictors, right?

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:03.540
And you also had some fun.

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:06.490
This was very new this
year with the llama models

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:10.690
and so on, to look at how LLMs
or very powerful predictors

00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:14.140
can be used as very
good compressors, right?

00:53:14.140 --> 00:53:18.970
And then we saw universal
compressors, right,

00:53:18.970 --> 00:53:22.120
where you don't even try to
model your data explicitly.

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:23.260
It's very implicit.

00:53:23.260 --> 00:53:25.420
Basically, you have
a compressor that

00:53:25.420 --> 00:53:30.170
achieves the entropy for entropy
rate for any stationary source,

00:53:30.170 --> 00:53:30.670
right?

00:53:30.670 --> 00:53:34.300
And we focused mostly
on LZ77, but we

00:53:34.300 --> 00:53:39.550
have projects on both LZ77 and
LZ78 as part of the projects.

00:53:39.550 --> 00:53:44.230
And the last thing we did
in lossless compression was

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:47.380
we looked at some tips on how
to use lossless compression

00:53:47.380 --> 00:53:49.150
in practice, right?

00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:51.910
So in future, I would
suggest whenever

00:53:51.910 --> 00:53:54.850
you have a problem
in your research,

00:53:54.850 --> 00:53:56.230
at your job,
whatever, right, you

00:53:56.230 --> 00:54:00.550
want to compress something, take
a look at those slides, right?

00:54:00.550 --> 00:54:06.350
Because oftentimes, we
see that people who are--

00:54:06.350 --> 00:54:09.350
now that you know
compression, if you remember,

00:54:09.350 --> 00:54:11.073
if you did that quiz
question, right,

00:54:11.073 --> 00:54:13.490
where you are asked to build
a compressor for your company

00:54:13.490 --> 00:54:17.160
and what do you do, so always
start from first principles,

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:17.660
right?

00:54:17.660 --> 00:54:20.600
Before you do anything
crazy, evaluate the thing,

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:23.930
understand how is
the standard doing?

00:54:23.930 --> 00:54:25.430
Do I have a model of the data?

00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:27.830
Can I compute the entropy?

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:31.310
Is there a gap between what
a standard compressor already

00:54:31.310 --> 00:54:33.680
gives me and what I
want to get to, right?

00:54:33.680 --> 00:54:35.930
Don't just start writing
your own compressor

00:54:35.930 --> 00:54:39.110
for every file you get, right?

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:43.280
And so one key idea which kept
repeating in the first half

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:45.590
and also in the second
half is that however

00:54:45.590 --> 00:54:49.183
complicated your data looks at
the first glance, what you want

00:54:49.183 --> 00:54:50.600
to do is you want
to model it, you

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:52.700
want to transform
it into something

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:55.730
that can be used with
standard compressors, right?

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:59.385
Try to reuse the tools,
don't re-implement

00:54:59.385 --> 00:55:01.010
your Huffman coders
and your arithmetic

00:55:01.010 --> 00:55:03.870
coders and your LZ77 coders.

00:55:03.870 --> 00:55:06.030
Of course, for projects,
you do that, that's fine.

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:11.130
But in real life, right, you
want to use tools that exist.

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:13.350
And then we switch to
lossy compression, right?

00:55:13.350 --> 00:55:19.420
So mostly Pulkit [INAUDIBLE]
and Saki talked about it.

00:55:19.420 --> 00:55:21.840
So we started with
some fundamentals.

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:24.510
We looked at
quantization, the simplest

00:55:24.510 --> 00:55:27.510
form of lossy compression.

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:30.360
Scalar quantization,
uniform scalar quantization.

00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:33.180
We looked at rate-distortion
theory, which

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:36.030
is the corresponding
thing to entropy,

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:40.067
and we understood the
fundamental limits there.

00:55:40.067 --> 00:55:42.150
And then we looked at
vector quantization and then

00:55:42.150 --> 00:55:46.380
we looked at transform coding
based on the intuition with--

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:48.480
we looked at that
eigenvector decomposition

00:55:48.480 --> 00:55:53.070
and tried to understand how to
decorrelate the input, right?

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:58.800
And we learned about DCT,
which is a transform that

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:00.900
works really well for images.

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:03.240
As you might have seen
if you didn't see now,

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:05.820
you will see later if
you ever try to use this,

00:56:05.820 --> 00:56:07.950
is that the theory
for lossy compression

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:10.710
is maybe not as
useful as the theory

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:13.200
for lossless compression.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:18.210
If I give you for lossless
compression for an IID source,

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:19.830
you know how to
achieve the entropy

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:23.700
within two bits using arithmetic
coding, for example, right?

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:26.760
But in lossy compression, there
is no such simple algorithms,

00:56:26.760 --> 00:56:27.990
unfortunately.

00:56:27.990 --> 00:56:30.528
So that's one thing.

00:56:30.528 --> 00:56:32.070
And the other thing
is you don't even

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:33.480
know the fundamental limit.

00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:36.240
If you go beyond the
Gaussian distribution,

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:38.040
you go to some
other distribution.

00:56:38.040 --> 00:56:40.770
Oftentimes, you can only
estimate the rate distortion

00:56:40.770 --> 00:56:42.030
function.

00:56:42.030 --> 00:56:45.240
However, it still gives you a
lot of good insights, right?

00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:47.460
And as we saw in
the design of JPEG,

00:56:47.460 --> 00:56:53.450
it was a very nice mix of theory
and human perception, right?

00:56:53.450 --> 00:56:55.460
So oftentimes, you
start with the theory

00:56:55.460 --> 00:56:58.970
then you bring in
human perception.

00:56:58.970 --> 00:56:59.900
Not anymore.

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:04.230
Nowadays, you just do machine
learning but in the olden days,

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:06.982
this is not correct, right?

00:57:06.982 --> 00:57:09.440
Even in machine learning, you
need to understand the theory

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:13.640
to do things, but yeah.

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:18.230
And then we looked at image
compression for a few lectures.

00:57:18.230 --> 00:57:19.400
So we looked at JPEG.

00:57:19.400 --> 00:57:22.190
We looked at ML-based
image compression.

00:57:22.190 --> 00:57:26.360
Some of you might have gotten
an intro to transforms signals

00:57:26.360 --> 00:57:29.920
and systems, DCT.

00:57:29.920 --> 00:57:34.300
And we looked at a demo
on audio compression.

00:57:34.300 --> 00:57:37.930
And today's lecture, we looked
at video compression, right?

00:57:37.930 --> 00:57:41.800
And as you might
have noticed, you

00:57:41.800 --> 00:57:44.230
keep seeing this block diagram
many, many times, right?

00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:48.640
So you start with something, you
transform it to decorrelate it,

00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:52.600
and then in the cases
of images or audio,

00:57:52.600 --> 00:57:54.790
you apply some sort
of quantization.

00:57:54.790 --> 00:57:57.970
In case of video, there
is this residual thing

00:57:57.970 --> 00:58:01.240
happening where you try
to subtract, compensate

00:58:01.240 --> 00:58:02.950
for the motion and so on.

00:58:02.950 --> 00:58:05.320
And after you do this
quantization, which

00:58:05.320 --> 00:58:07.090
is the only lossy
part of the equation,

00:58:07.090 --> 00:58:08.800
you do some lossless coding.

00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:11.200
And here, you can use any
of your lossless coders.

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:17.410
And in fact, PNG uses LZ77, JPEG
uses Huffman coding and so on.

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:20.143
The new ones use
arithmetic coding or ANS.

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:27.050
And it's a big,
big topic, right?

00:58:27.050 --> 00:58:30.230
Even the things we covered, we
couldn't cover in full detail.

00:58:30.230 --> 00:58:33.620
Video compression, this was just
a very initial basic flavor.

00:58:33.620 --> 00:58:35.870
Image compression, we just
talked mostly about JPEG,

00:58:35.870 --> 00:58:39.710
but JPEG is now 20 years old.

00:58:39.710 --> 00:58:42.590
And there were topics that
are very exciting that we

00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:44.180
didn't even get to.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:47.990
So for example,
distributed compression.

00:58:47.990 --> 00:58:50.420
If you have multiple
sensors, multiple nodes,

00:58:50.420 --> 00:58:53.300
they want to compress
their own data

00:58:53.300 --> 00:58:55.160
but their data
depends on each other.

00:58:55.160 --> 00:58:59.570
So how can they compress
it together, right?

00:58:59.570 --> 00:59:01.760
And actually, the puzzle
in homework three,

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:05.660
the one that you wanted to
send X and the other guy

00:59:05.660 --> 00:59:08.420
had Y gives you some
sense of how to do this.

00:59:08.420 --> 00:59:10.550
And this actually ties
into the other part

00:59:10.550 --> 00:59:13.520
of information theory, which
is error correction coding.

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:15.350
And the tools from
error correction coding

00:59:15.350 --> 00:59:18.800
are used for distributed
compression very often.

00:59:18.800 --> 00:59:21.740
Another thing we didn't look
at as much as succinct data

00:59:21.740 --> 00:59:24.840
structures, which is--

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:27.503
so the way we thought
about compression

00:59:27.503 --> 00:59:28.920
throughout the
class was you start

00:59:28.920 --> 00:59:30.003
with an uncompressed file.

00:59:30.003 --> 00:59:30.780
You compress it.

00:59:30.780 --> 00:59:33.480
You keep it in cold storage,
then you bring it out then

00:59:33.480 --> 00:59:34.620
you decompress it, right?

00:59:36.848 --> 00:59:39.390
I guess, the first time you saw
something different was today

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:43.020
in case of videos, where you
saw that you have a large video

00:59:43.020 --> 00:59:45.820
file on YouTube,
and you certainly

00:59:45.820 --> 00:59:48.280
want to seek to a particular
spot in the video.

00:59:48.280 --> 00:59:50.680
You don't want to decompress
the whole video to do that.

00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:53.620
You want some level of
random access to the video.

00:59:53.620 --> 00:59:56.620
This happens a lot, right?

00:59:56.620 --> 00:59:58.240
I've seen it in
genomics a lot where

00:59:58.240 --> 01:00:00.970
you have a lot of genomic data
and you want to search over

01:00:00.970 --> 01:00:03.340
the data very quickly,
but you also want

01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:04.660
to store it compressed, right?

01:00:04.660 --> 01:00:07.930
So you want to get the
benefits of compression

01:00:07.930 --> 01:00:11.920
without losing your ability
to randomly access the data,

01:00:11.920 --> 01:00:14.860
or do some operations
on the data, right?

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:16.660
So this is just
like one big field

01:00:16.660 --> 01:00:18.370
of succinct data structures.

01:00:21.340 --> 01:00:25.060
And one thing we saw a
little but not as much as--

01:00:27.750 --> 01:00:32.730
I think [? Saki ?] likes to call
it compression for hardware,

01:00:32.730 --> 01:00:35.520
in hardware, or compression
for neural networks,

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:37.690
of neural networks,
by neural networks.

01:00:37.690 --> 01:00:40.080
So I guess, we did
see compression

01:00:40.080 --> 01:00:41.760
using neural networks, right?

01:00:41.760 --> 01:00:43.560
But there is a
corresponding thing

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:45.810
where you have a
neural network model.

01:00:45.810 --> 01:00:49.590
Some of you might have been
following the recent LLM

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:51.660
trend might have seen
that people are trying

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:53.320
to quantize the models, right?

01:00:53.320 --> 01:00:56.010
They're trying to sparsify the
models to all sorts of things

01:00:56.010 --> 01:00:57.720
to make the models
smaller, right?

01:00:57.720 --> 01:01:01.290
And oftentimes, various ideas
from compression are useful,

01:01:01.290 --> 01:01:04.930
they're also from optimization.

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:07.370
So you want to do it in a way
that does not lose accuracy.

01:01:07.370 --> 01:01:09.250
So it's a form of
lossy compression

01:01:09.250 --> 01:01:13.090
where the distortion is not
human perception, right?

01:01:13.090 --> 01:01:15.130
There is no human
perception related

01:01:15.130 --> 01:01:17.350
to the weights of
a neural network,

01:01:17.350 --> 01:01:19.943
but the distortion is the
accuracy of the model.

01:01:19.943 --> 01:01:21.610
You want to lossily
compress it in a way

01:01:21.610 --> 01:01:24.710
that it does not
lose its accuracy.

01:01:24.710 --> 01:01:26.810
Another thing is
compression in hardware.

01:01:26.810 --> 01:01:29.790
So we saw today that the
Apple chips, for example,

01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:32.480
or most of these processors
have a dedicated video coding

01:01:32.480 --> 01:01:34.280
hardware.

01:01:34.280 --> 01:01:36.740
Similarly, Z standard,
one of the reasons

01:01:36.740 --> 01:01:38.570
it's so much faster
than gzip is that it

01:01:38.570 --> 01:01:40.640
can use dedicated instructions.

01:01:40.640 --> 01:01:43.580
It can use special
same-day instructions,

01:01:43.580 --> 01:01:47.730
so that's a big
field, basically.

01:01:47.730 --> 01:01:51.180
How do you use the existing
instructions on your processor

01:01:51.180 --> 01:01:52.472
to make compression faster?

01:01:52.472 --> 01:01:54.930
We have some projects which
are doing compression on a GPU,

01:01:54.930 --> 01:01:56.940
for example.

01:01:56.940 --> 01:02:00.480
And the corresponding thing
is, if you have a compression

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:03.300
problem, can you then
design specialized hardware

01:02:03.300 --> 01:02:05.790
that can improve that
particular algorithm?

01:02:08.570 --> 01:02:12.110
And then we mostly stuck to
general purpose compression,

01:02:12.110 --> 01:02:15.170
except for image and
video, which is very big.

01:02:15.170 --> 01:02:18.560
But there are a lot of work
on special domains, right?

01:02:18.560 --> 01:02:21.830
So AR/VR is one.

01:02:21.830 --> 01:02:26.240
I think Pulkit
briefly mentioned it.

01:02:26.240 --> 01:02:28.100
If you have this
virtual reality headset,

01:02:28.100 --> 01:02:30.860
right, you are transmitting huge
amounts of data to the headset.

01:02:30.860 --> 01:02:34.280
It's a 3D model of
the thing, and you

01:02:34.280 --> 01:02:37.040
want to be very intentional
about how you compress it,

01:02:37.040 --> 01:02:37.580
right?

01:02:37.580 --> 01:02:41.270
If the person is looking at
this spot in their VR thing,

01:02:41.270 --> 01:02:42.687
you use more bits
on the part they

01:02:42.687 --> 01:02:44.395
are looking at and
less bits for the part

01:02:44.395 --> 01:02:45.440
they're not looking at.

01:02:45.440 --> 01:02:51.440
So people optimized very fine
grained way to get the best.

01:02:51.440 --> 01:02:54.470
Basically, you want the
headset to not heat up

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:58.700
and the wires to not be
super thick and so on, right?

01:02:58.700 --> 01:03:02.390
So compression plays
a big role, right?

01:03:02.390 --> 01:03:04.220
I don't know about
the Vision Pro,

01:03:04.220 --> 01:03:06.770
maybe Kedar can tell
us about some secrets,

01:03:06.770 --> 01:03:10.715
but I'm sure there was some
compression somewhere in there.

01:03:13.940 --> 01:03:16.820
Similarly, genomics, right?

01:03:16.820 --> 01:03:19.610
It's a very rapidly
growing field.

01:03:19.610 --> 01:03:26.410
And I got my opportunity to work
on genomics for most of my PhD.

01:03:26.410 --> 01:03:28.540
Lots of very interesting
applications there,

01:03:28.540 --> 01:03:31.180
both in terms of
compressing things

01:03:31.180 --> 01:03:33.340
and sometimes
losslessly, sometimes

01:03:33.340 --> 01:03:35.110
lossily compressing things.

01:03:35.110 --> 01:03:39.010
And also, being able to
compress while maintaining

01:03:39.010 --> 01:03:43.600
random access, so succinct data
structures and so on, right?

01:03:43.600 --> 01:03:46.450
People here I think are working
on a few different domains.

01:03:46.450 --> 01:03:51.250
We will see on Wednesday
with the projects.

01:03:51.250 --> 01:03:55.240
So yeah, some part we got to,
some part we didn't get to.

01:03:55.240 --> 01:03:58.750
But hopefully, you enjoyed it.

01:03:58.750 --> 01:04:02.200
So what next?

01:04:02.200 --> 01:04:05.320
So if you liked the
class, even if you

01:04:05.320 --> 01:04:07.510
didn't like, in any case,
there are these resources

01:04:07.510 --> 01:04:08.290
available to you.

01:04:08.290 --> 01:04:10.225
One is this Stanford
Compression Library.

01:04:14.310 --> 01:04:16.975
You have used it
for your homework.

01:04:16.975 --> 01:04:18.600
There are parts you
might have not seen

01:04:18.600 --> 01:04:22.110
as part of the
homework, so we will

01:04:22.110 --> 01:04:24.720
try to keep improving
it over the years.

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:27.360
And hopefully, some of you,
your projects we want to

01:04:27.360 --> 01:04:28.637
include as--

01:04:28.637 --> 01:04:30.720
basically, if you create
a merge request and we do

01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:35.130
want to include some of
that in the library itself.

01:04:35.130 --> 01:04:36.960
And the whole idea
is to make something

01:04:36.960 --> 01:04:43.290
that's easy for people to
use and easy for people

01:04:43.290 --> 01:04:47.160
to use experiment with
and understand, right?

01:04:47.160 --> 01:04:49.470
A lot of it started with us
wanting to understand how

01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:52.740
arithmetic coding or ANS
or range coding works,

01:04:52.740 --> 01:04:58.170
and running into very optimized
C code where people don't even

01:04:58.170 --> 01:05:00.060
want to write--

01:05:00.060 --> 01:05:04.440
they don't even write and like
a normal beginner programmer.

01:05:04.440 --> 01:05:08.370
They just combine everything
into a weird bitmask thing,

01:05:08.370 --> 01:05:10.605
very, very hard to interpret
a lot of that code.

01:05:13.120 --> 01:05:15.100
So we made an effort,
and it's been useful

01:05:15.100 --> 01:05:17.170
for us to teach the
class, if nothing else,

01:05:17.170 --> 01:05:21.400
and hopefully, it's
useful to some of you.

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:23.020
The website has some resources.

01:05:23.020 --> 01:05:23.920
Take a look.

01:05:23.920 --> 01:05:25.765
We hope to keep
updating it over time.

01:05:29.190 --> 01:05:31.590
And then there are other
classes at Stanford which

01:05:31.590 --> 01:05:33.910
you might find interesting.

01:05:33.910 --> 01:05:39.960
So EE276, information theory,
the first few lectures

01:05:39.960 --> 01:05:42.120
of this class will be
common, but then, there

01:05:42.120 --> 01:05:43.080
is a lot more theory.

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:46.380
You will get much
better at the basics.

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:49.230
[? Saki, ?] are
you teaching this?

01:05:49.230 --> 01:05:52.848
So [? Saki ?] will be
teaching it this year, right?

01:05:52.848 --> 01:05:54.390
And especially, in
lossy compression,

01:05:54.390 --> 01:05:57.045
I think you will see
much more theory.

01:06:02.350 --> 01:06:05.710
EE376 topics in
information theory.

01:06:05.710 --> 01:06:09.760
There used to be a
series E, 376 ABC.

01:06:09.760 --> 01:06:12.730
376C was the first
course I took at Stanford

01:06:12.730 --> 01:06:13.960
when I came to Stanford.

01:06:13.960 --> 01:06:17.290
It was called universal schemes
in information theory, where

01:06:17.290 --> 01:06:19.720
we actually proved
that LZ78 achieves

01:06:19.720 --> 01:06:23.380
the entropy rate for stationary
sources and even more

01:06:23.380 --> 01:06:28.480
powerful finite results.

01:06:28.480 --> 01:06:32.680
I don't know if
there is a plan to--

01:06:32.680 --> 01:06:33.715
is there plan?

01:06:33.715 --> 01:06:35.020
There is a plan but--

01:06:35.020 --> 01:06:35.995
OK.

01:06:35.995 --> 01:06:37.300
[INAUDIBLE]

01:06:37.300 --> 01:06:38.980
OK, OK, so yeah.

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:44.460
If you are at Stanford when
that happens, take a look.

01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:47.670
For those who are
interested in music audio,

01:06:47.670 --> 01:06:51.390
there is a class
called Music 422 which

01:06:51.390 --> 01:06:53.120
I think is running this year.

01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:56.360
Please check, right?

01:06:56.360 --> 01:06:59.340
We looked at a bit of audio
coding in that one notebook

01:06:59.340 --> 01:07:00.750
that Pulkit showed.

01:07:00.750 --> 01:07:05.340
But there is much more, there's
a lot of psycho acoustics type

01:07:05.340 --> 01:07:08.790
things, a lot of human
perception comes in.

01:07:08.790 --> 01:07:13.500
And if you look at the mp3 or
AAC or any of these codecs,

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:15.360
they are pretty complicated.

01:07:15.360 --> 01:07:18.930
They do a lot of things
to optimize things.

01:07:18.930 --> 01:07:22.110
And in fact, audio coding
is also one of those domains

01:07:22.110 --> 01:07:25.350
where recently, machine
learning-based methods,

01:07:25.350 --> 01:07:30.780
particularly from Facebook, have
been very, very influential.

01:07:30.780 --> 01:07:34.470
And finally this class, which
is not strictly on compression,

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:38.370
it's on modeling probabilistic
graphical models.

01:07:38.370 --> 01:07:43.170
And I would say, in today's
day and age, even CS 236

01:07:43.170 --> 01:07:46.510
which is on generative
models in AI,

01:07:46.510 --> 01:07:49.473
those might be useful for
you because we talked about,

01:07:49.473 --> 01:07:50.890
if you have a good
model, then you

01:07:50.890 --> 01:07:52.030
can build a good compressor.

01:07:52.030 --> 01:07:53.447
But how do you get
the good model?

01:07:53.447 --> 01:07:55.510
How do you actually
find a good model?

01:07:55.510 --> 01:07:58.810
And that usually comes in terms
of either this sort of more

01:07:58.810 --> 01:08:01.240
probabilistic techniques or
more machine learning type

01:08:01.240 --> 01:08:02.845
techniques, right?

01:08:06.190 --> 01:08:06.760
So, yeah.

01:08:06.760 --> 01:08:09.670
So my journey in compression
basically started in 2016

01:08:09.670 --> 01:08:12.430
when I joined [? Saki's ?] lab.

01:08:12.430 --> 01:08:15.040
I had no idea I was going
to do compression in my PhD.

01:08:15.040 --> 01:08:18.930
He gave me a problem,
I started with it.

01:08:18.930 --> 01:08:22.080
It's coming to an end
now, but hopefully, I

01:08:22.080 --> 01:08:26.279
will come back to it and I
will be in touch, definitely.

01:08:26.279 --> 01:08:27.662
So thank you.

01:08:27.662 --> 01:08:28.620
Hopefully, you enjoyed.

01:08:28.620 --> 01:08:33.380
Thank you to all the instructors
and to the students and ITA.

